  IS g IN THE CANDIDATE SET?  ASKED OF THE LAW AND OF THE SURVEY

  1. WHAT THE LAW ITSELF ADMITS
     The admissibility test is q < 2(2l+1).  An empty 5g has q = 0 and passes it.
     Nothing in the law's statement mentions l, so excluding g is an extra clause
     that has to come from somewhere other than the law.

  2. WHAT ADMITTING g COSTS, over the 106 steps
     g candidates offered:        80 step-pairs
     of those, BINDING:           16
       giving exactly a > 0:      11  -- the parameter's own domain, no information
       giving a real bound:       5
          Rf  104  entrant 6d  vs 5g   a > 0.5773503   (= 1/sqrt(3))
          Db  105  entrant 6d  vs 5g   a > 0.5773503   (= 1/sqrt(3))
          Sg  106  entrant 6d  vs 5g   a > 0.5773503   (= 1/sqrt(3))
          Bh  107  entrant 6d  vs 5g   a > 0.5773503   (= 1/sqrt(3))
          Hs  108  entrant 6d  vs 5g   a > 0.5773503   (= 1/sqrt(3))
     corridors non-empty, l <= 3: 106 of 106
     corridors non-empty, l <= 4: 106 of 106
     a g subshell wins the step:  NEVER
     So admitting g never changes which subshell the law selects and never
     empties a corridor.  It moves the FLOOR at sixteen steps and nothing else.

