       Sr I      2[5/2]             4       5-9   0.03587
       Al I      2G                 3       5-7   0.00841
     4 of the 12 g channels carry ten or more members;
     the deepest runs n = 8 to 25 over twenty-nine of them.  A twenty-nine-member
     series with a defect of 0.05 is not a channel sitting at hydrogenic depth.

  4. CHAPTER 35's CRITERION, TESTED ON THE SURVEY
     'every g channel ... sits at its hydrogenic depth to the storage precision
      ... a channel that does not respond to the nucleus is not in the field.'
     A quantum defect of zero IS hydrogenic depth.  Measured, by l:
         l  measured  |delta| min  |delta| max     median
         s        92      0.00030      5.07221    1.03980
         p        81      0.00020      4.59521    0.69910
         d        89      0.00020      3.11838    0.11090
         f        59      0.00000      1.12064    0.02480
         g        36      0.00000      0.04010    0.00505
         h         1      0.00013      0.00013    0.00013
         i         0
         ?         0
     g defects run to 0.04010, and 24 of 36 exceed 0.001.  They are small and they are NOT zero.
     r(Z, |delta|) over the measured g channels = +0.330, so they
     rise with the nucleus -- which is the corpus's own high-l mechanism, the
     defect following core polarisability beyond l = 3.
     The l-collapse ladder above is the corpus's own law reproduced: the median
     defect falls monotonically from s to h across six orders of magnitude.

  5. WHAT THIS SETTLES
     g channels EXIST and are MEASURED, in thirty-six species, thirty-two of them
     ions.  So 'no observation distinguishes the two candidate sets' was wrong,
     and 'g does not respond to the nucleus' is not what the survey shows.
     Neither ground for excluding g survives the data.

  6. WHAT IS STILL M's, and it is narrower than it was
     A measured g channel is a RYDBERG channel.  No element below Z = 121 has a g
     electron in its GROUND configuration, and the law is about ground occupancy.
     So the question is no longer 'does g exist' -- it does -- but 'may the law
     consider a channel that exists and is never the ground entrant'.
     The law's own test admits it; the ground data never exercises it; and the
     cost of admitting it is one moved floor at sixteen steps, eleven of which
     move it only to a > 0.  Nothing is repaired here.
